"""Original fictional aggregate exercises; no real survivor data or copied tools."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-056","Ethical GBV Data and Programme Evaluation","ethical-gbv-data-and-programme-evaluation","Ethical GBV Data\nand Programme Evaluation",
 "Critique a fictional GBV data and evaluation proposal through purpose limitation, confidentiality, defined measures and appropriately bounded conclusions, without collecting real survivor information.",[
m("Purpose consent and the information lifecycle","Distinguish information purposes and specify a minimum-data governance outline.",[
"Begin with the decision or service purpose, not a list of information that might someday be useful. Sensitive data create responsibilities at collection, recording, access, storage, analysis, sharing, retention and disposal. Ask who needs each field for the stated purpose and whether a less identifying alternative would suffice. The October 2026 UNFPA guidance addresses GBV service data in integrated programming; it does not authorise learners to conduct research or obtain case files. This course uses fictional aggregate exercise data only and never requires incident narratives, contact details or actual service records. [S19, S1]",
"Health records and GBV case-management information serve distinct functions and operate under different professional arrangements. Co-location, coordination or a shared funding source does not automatically authorise combining them. A provider's legitimate need for a specific referral detail is not permission to receive an entire case file. Actual systems need approved governance, purpose-specific access and appropriate agreements, with qualified roles handling clinical, safeguarding and legal questions. In a fictional analysis, name the separate purposes and identify the authority that must resolve a sharing request rather than design an unrestricted shared spreadsheet. [S19, S10]",
"Informed consent concerns a specific understood purpose and relevant choices, benefits, risks and limits. Consent to receive help is not automatic consent to research, publicity or cross-service analysis. Do not promise confidentiality without exceptions or invent a universal rule for reporting or emergency disclosure. Applicable law, organisational safeguarding procedures, the person's circumstances and qualified decisions matter, particularly for children. A learner can identify where expert review is required; the worksheet does not grant permission to collect information or decide a real exception because the proposed use seems beneficial. [S19, S1]",
"Privacy risks can remain after names are removed. A combination of location, timing, role or a rare circumstance may identify someone, and even an aggregate figure may be sensitive in context. Limit access and outputs to the approved purpose, assess indirect identification and use the organisation's authorised security and retention arrangements. A fictional governance note should state responsible roles, an access boundary, a retention decision requiring approval and a process for concerns or incidents. It should not specify a universal retention period, certify legal compliance or treat a password alone as a complete information-protection system. [S19, S20, S10]"],
"Fictional case: a co-located clinic and GBV service propose combining all records for a sponsor dashboard. A learner distinguishes clinical and case-management purposes, asks what the dashboard decision needs and identifies a qualified governance review before any sharing.",
"Write a minimum-data governance note with purpose, unnecessary fields, distinct record types, consent limits, indirect-identification concern, responsible authority and an unresolved retention question. Use no real records.",
"A defensible note begins with purpose, separates clinical and casework information and avoids assuming coordination permits unrestricted linkage. Consent and confidentiality limits require appropriate qualified decisions.",["S19","S20","S10","S1"],[
("Data collection should follow a justified purpose.","A fictional dashboard requests every available field for possible future use. What is the first correction?","Define the decision and collect only information justified for that purpose.",["Copy all records before deciding what is useful.","Assume more identifying detail always improves safety.","Treat sponsor interest as complete data-collection authority."],"Purpose and minimisation should precede information gathering."),
("Clinical and GBV case records have distinct functions.","A clinic and a GBV service share a building. What does this alone authorise?","No automatic sharing or linkage of their full records.",["Unrestricted access to all clinical and case notes.","A public combined survivor spreadsheet.","Research use without separate governance."],"Co-location does not remove professional purpose, access and consent boundaries."),
("Service consent does not automatically permit research.","A fictional client agrees to a support referral. What should not be inferred?","Permission for unrelated research, promotion or unrestricted secondary analysis.",["That the referral purpose has limits.","That confidentiality needs an appropriate explanation.","That only necessary information should be considered."],"A specific service choice cannot supply blanket consent for other uses."),
("Removing names does not guarantee anonymity.","A table omits names but identifies a unique role and small location. What remains necessary?","Assess contextual identification and reporting risks before approved sharing.",["Declare it anonymous solely because the name column is gone.","Publish it automatically as aggregate data.","Assume every indirect identifier is harmless."],"Combinations of details may remain identifying."),
("Legal and safeguarding exceptions require qualified handling.","A learner wants to decide a real reporting exception using the course worksheet. What boundary applies?","Use designated qualified procedures and context-specific advice; the worksheet is not authorisation.",["Any beneficial purpose overrides all privacy limits.","One universal rule covers every jurisdiction and child.","A high quiz score creates legal decision authority."],"The course identifies governance questions rather than deciding actual exceptions."),
]),
m("Counts denominators and service indicators","Calculate fictional proportions and state what service data can and cannot establish.",[
"An indicator needs an explicit definition: what is counted, the unit, the period, the eligible group, the source and how missing information is handled. Visits, referrals offered, referrals accepted and unique people are different units. A person may use a service more than once or appear in more than one provider's figures. An apparently simple total can therefore double-count, omit groups or conceal uncertainty. Do not remove those uncertainties by quietly assuming every record is a different person or that every offer resulted in support accessed. [S20, S19]",
"Consider an invented exercise in which completed referrals are twelve of thirty eligible referrals in one period and eighteen of sixty in another. The corresponding proportions are forty percent and thirty percent. The completed count increased while the proportion decreased by ten percentage points. State both numerator and denominator and do not call that a ten percent relative decrease. Comparability still depends on definitions, periods and the eligible groups. These fictional aggregates are for arithmetic and interpretation; they are not real service performance, evidence of violence change or a target for judging individual staff. [S20, S19]",
"Service data concern people who reached particular services under particular recording conditions. They are not population prevalence data. Higher recorded use may reflect improved access, changing capacity, altered definitions, repeated contacts or other factors, and cannot by itself establish that violence increased. Lower use may reflect barriers rather than reduced need. The 2026 guidance explicitly distinguishes service information from prevalence research. A learner should state that limit clearly rather than divide a service total by a convenient community population and label the result the local rate of GBV. [S19, S10]",
"Missing information is an evidence limitation, not a zero. If follow-up is unavailable, a referral outcome is unknown rather than automatically completed or failed. Explain coverage without attempting to fill gaps through unapproved survivor contact, record linkage or investigation. Any real analytic request needs qualified data governance, purpose-specific permissions and appropriate safety review. A fictional indicator note should show the calculation, denominator, relevant uncertainty and a bounded review question, with only the minimum approved reporting detail. Clean-looking tables do not compensate for an unsuitable measure or unsafe information process. [S20, S19, S1]"],
"Fictional dataset: period A has twelve completed referrals among thirty eligible referrals; period B has eighteen among sixty. No individual records are supplied. A draft report says the completion rate rose because eighteen exceeds twelve.",
"Calculate both proportions and their percentage-point difference. Correct the report, identify two comparability questions, distinguish unknown follow-up from zero and explain why these figures cannot estimate GBV prevalence.",
"A is 40% and B is 30%, a decrease of 10 percentage points despite a higher completed count. Definitions, coverage and period comparability remain to check. Service referrals do not measure population prevalence or causal programme impact.",["S20","S19","S10","S1"],[
("Counts and proportions can move in different directions.","Completed referrals change from twelve of thirty to eighteen of sixty in a fictional exercise. What are the proportions?","Forty percent followed by thirty percent.",["Thirty percent followed by forty percent.","Twelve percent followed by eighteen percent.","Both are sixty percent because the second count is larger."],"12/30 is 0.40 and 18/60 is 0.30."),
("Percentage points differ from relative percent change.","A fictional completion proportion changes from 40% to 30%. What is the absolute percentage-point change?","A decrease of ten percentage points.",["An increase of ten percentage points.","A decrease of ten percent relative to the original rate.","No change because both figures are percentages."],"Subtracting proportions gives ten percentage points; relative change is a different calculation."),
("Service use is not population prevalence.","Recorded GBV service visits rise in one month. What can be concluded from that count alone?","Recorded visits rose; population violence prevalence and the cause of the change remain unestablished.",["Violence prevalence necessarily rose by the same amount.","Every visit represents a different person.","The programme necessarily caused increased violence."],"Use depends on access, definitions, repeat contacts and other conditions."),
("Unknown follow-up is not zero or confirmed success.","A fictional referral record has no follow-up outcome. How should it be represented in a review?","As unknown under a defined missing-data rule, with coverage stated.",["Automatically as successful completion.","Automatically as a failure caused by the learner.","As permission to contact the person through any available channel."],"Missing evidence should remain visible without unsafe gap-filling."),
("Comparable indicators need defined units and eligibility.","Two services report twenty contacts and twenty unique clients. What should an analyst avoid?","Treating the units as interchangeable without checking definitions and repeat use.",["Stating the reporting period.","Clarifying eligible groups.","Explaining a possible double-counting limitation."],"Contacts and distinct people are different analytic units."),
]),
m("Evaluation design feedback and bounded conclusions","Match a review question to appropriate evidence and an accountable response.",[
"Monitoring describes delivery and relevant changes over time; evaluation asks structured questions about a programme's design, implementation or results. Begin with a question that can be answered safely within the available authority and resources. A service-access review is different from a study of violence reduction, and both differ from an individual clinical assessment. For coursework, critique a fictional proposal rather than recruit participants, administer a sensitive instrument or request private files. Actual GBV research and consultation require qualified ethical, safeguarding, methodological and service arrangements beyond an introductory course. [S20, S10, S1]",
"A measure should match the construct and population. A questionnaire focused on technology-facilitated sexual violence cannot automatically represent every form of GBV or all aspects of service quality. The 2026 measurement review maps 218 measures across 319 studies but does not appraise study quality or establish robustness. Its heavily Western high-income coverage limits transfer. Selection for an actual study would require appropriate validation, cultural and language suitability, ethical review and permissions; appearing in an open-access review does not license copying an instrument or prove that it is suitable for Nigeria. [R28, S1]",
"Interpret findings in line with the design. A before/after difference without an appropriate comparison may reflect changing groups, reporting, service capacity or wider conditions. A satisfaction response is one perspective, not proof of recovery or absence of violence. Missing perspectives and feedback conditions matter: pressure to praise a provider can distort a review, while a small-group summary may expose identity. Do not treat a larger number of records as a substitute for safe collection and relevant evidence. Report the question, source, coverage, method and limitations so an authorised reader can assess the actual claim. [S20, S19, R28]",
"An evaluation is useful when it informs a proportionate decision and an accountable response. State what should change, who can authorise it, a resource dependency and a point to review the response. If evidence is insufficient, say what remains unknown rather than claim success or failure beyond the measure. Feedback should be offered through appropriate safe routes and should not be linked to continued access to support. This course develops critical interpretation using fictional material; it does not authorise real GBV data collection, approve a research protocol or replace independent curriculum and supervised skills review. [S10, S20, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a service claims recovery from a higher referral count and proposes copying a foreign digital-abuse questionnaire into an unapproved survey. A learner separates the claims, identifies measure and ethical-review gaps and recommends a bounded authorised service-access review.",
"Write an evaluation brief with question, appropriate evidence type, unsuitable inference, measure-transfer concern, missing-perspective check and an action owner. State what requires qualified approval before real collection.",
"A defensible brief matches evidence to the question, recognises validation and design limits and avoids unapproved collection or copied instruments. Its recommendation identifies authority and follow-through without claiming causal recovery.",["S20","S10","S1","S19","R28"],[
("The review question determines the evidence needed.","A fictional team wants to assess service access but proposes an unrelated violence-prevalence claim. What should be clarified first?","The actual question, relevant construct, ethical scope and feasible method.",["Which unrelated headline will attract most attention.","How to collect the maximum private detail.","How to label all monitoring as a clinical assessment."],"Different questions require different evidence and safeguards."),
("An open-access article does not validate or license every instrument it lists.","A listed foreign questionnaire is proposed for a Nigerian GBV survey. What must be considered before actual use?","Construct fit, local validation, permissions and qualified ethical and safeguarding review.",["Only the journal logo.","Automatic validity because the review is recent.","An assumption that all listed instruments are unrestricted."],"Evidence mapping, validation and instrument rights are separate questions."),
("A before/after service change has alternative explanations.","A fictional service records more referrals after a campaign. What limits a causal-effect claim?","Changes in access, recording, groups or wider conditions may also explain the observation.",["The calendar sequence alone proves causation.","More records guarantee less violence.","An attractive chart removes confounding."],"An uncontrolled observation cannot identify the programme as the sole cause."),
("Feedback conditions affect interpretation.","People are told that positive feedback helps them retain support. What is wrong with the proposed review?","The pressure undermines voluntary feedback and may distort findings.",["It guarantees unbiased satisfaction data.","It makes confidentiality unnecessary.","It proves every respondent recovered."],"Feedback should not be coerced or linked to continued assistance."),
("Evaluation findings should lead to proportionate accountable action.","A fictional review identifies an unresolved access barrier. Which recommendation is useful?","An authorised owner, feasible corrective action, resource condition and review point with limits stated.",["A universal success claim without evidence.","Publishing case details to create pressure.","Collecting more sensitive records without approval."],"Accountability connects a supported finding to an appropriate response."),
])])
